# Git

Trunk-based. `main` is always shippable. Short-lived branches. PR into `main`. Squash merge. Do not force-push `main`.

## Branch

```
<type>/<adr-or-issue>-<short-description>
```

- `type`: `feat` `fix` `docs` `refactor` `test` `chore` `ci` `perf` `revert` `rfc`
- `adr-or-issue`: ADR number (`0001` or `adr-0001`) when implementing an accepted RFC; otherwise the GitHub issue number.
- `short-description`: lowercase kebab-case, few words.
- One ADR (or one issue) per branch. Do not pile unrelated work.

Examples: `feat/0001-session-store`, `fix/42-null-user`, `rfc/0003-rate-limits`, `chore/12-gitignore`

Trivial loop-skip (typo, one-liner, no ticket): `chore/skip-<slug>`.

Create the branch before `/implement`. Never commit RFC/ADR/plan work on `main`.

## Commit

[Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/):

```
<type>(<optional-scope>): <description>
```

- Imperative, lowercase description, no trailing period.
- `scope` optional: area (`auth`, `rfc`, `harness`).
- Breaking change: put `!` after type or after `)`: `feat(auth)!: replace sessions with jwt`
- Body optional. Footer `BREAKING CHANGE: <what>` only when `!` is used and the human needs the migration note.
- One logical change per commit. Do not mix format-only with behavior.

Examples:

```
feat(auth): persist sessions in redis
fix(api): reject empty user id
docs(rfc): add success criteria to 0001
feat(auth)!: drop cookie sessions
```

## Pull request

Title matches the squash commit (`type(scope): description`). Body links RFC and ADR paths. CI must be green.
